[49m[39m  80,  80[0m:[39m[49m ## Rows deliberately absent
[0m[49m[39m  81,  81[0m:[39m[49m 
[0m[49m[39m  82,  82[0m:[39m[49m There is no row for buffer sizes, element counts, endianness selection, output
[0m[49m[39m  83,  83[0m:[39m[49m formats, encoding modes, thread counts, or init/config structs, because the C API
[0m[49m[39m  84,  84[0m:[39m[49m has no parameter or branch for any of them. Inventing such rows would be guessing
[0m[49m[39m  85,  85[0m:[39m[49m rather than deriving from the source.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+      86[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+      87[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ## What "passes" means per row
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+      88[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+      89[0m:[38;5;10m[49m Rows 1–11 and 14–15, 17–18 are compared **byte for byte** between the two
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+      90[0m:[38;5;10m[49m libraries. Rows 12, 13 and 16 (the arms that reach the uninitialised read on an
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+      91[0m:[38;5;10m[49m uncontrolled slot) are compared on **termination status plus output shape**,
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+      92[0m:[38;5;10m[49m because the C library's own output for those is a leaked stack address that
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+      93[0m:[38;5;10m[49m changes on every run under ASLR — byte equality is not a property the C has
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+      94[0m:[38;5;10m[49m against itself. That distinction is confined to exactly those rows; rows 14 and
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+      95[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 15 reach the *same* defect on a slot a predecessor deliberately wrote, and there
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+      96[0m:[38;5;10m[49m byte equality is asserted and holds.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+      97[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+      98[0m:[38;5;10m[49m Measured stability of the two comparison-by-termination cases (40 isolated runs
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+      99[0m:[38;5;10m[49m each, both libraries):
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     100[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     101[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | spec | C survives | C faults | Rust survives | Rust faults |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     102[0m:[38;5;10m[49m |------|-----------|----------|---------------|-------------|
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     103[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | `driver(0)` | 40 | 0 | 40 | 0 |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     104[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | `bad()` (from the test harness's frame) | 0 | 40 | 0 | 40 |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     105[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     106[0m:[38;5;10m[49m So the two agree on termination deterministically; it is only the printed
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     107[0m:[38;5;10m[49m garbage value that is unspecified.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     108[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     109[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ## A divergence this table found
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     110[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     111[0m:[38;5;10m[49m The first translation implemented `driver` as an idiomatic Rust `if`/`else`.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     112[0m:[38;5;10m[49m LLVM compiled that to a **tail jump** to `bad`, whereas the C emits a stack frame
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     113[0m:[38;5;10m[49m plus a real `call`. `bad` therefore read a stack slot 32 bytes higher than in the
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     114[0m:[38;5;10m[49m C, and printed a small stale value (`3`) where the C printed a leaked stack
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     115[0m:[38;5;10m[49m address (`603893760`, differing every run). Row 12 catches this; rows 16 and 17
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     116[0m:[38;5;10m[49m catch it too. It was fixed by emitting all four functions as naked functions
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     117[0m:[38;5;10m[49m carrying the C's `-O0` codegen verbatim — see `src/lib.rs`.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     118[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     119[0m:[38;5;10m[49m The second divergence was linkage rather than codegen: a Rust `cdylib` is linked
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     120[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `-z now` by default while the C `.so` is lazily bound, and the first PLT call in
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     121[0m:[38;5;10m[49m the C runs `_dl_runtime_resolve` straight through the slot `bad` reads.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     122[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `.cargo/config.toml` passes `-Wl,-z,lazy` to match. Row 17 and
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     123[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `tests/symbols.rs::lazy_binding_matches_the_c_library` both catch a regression
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     124[0m:[38;5;10m[49m here (verified by removing `.cargo/config.toml` and relinking).
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     125[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     126[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ## Test-harness notes
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     127[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     128[0m:[38;5;10m[49m * The test targets are `harness = false`. Comparing the libraries means capturing
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     129[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   fd 1, and libtest's own progress output ("ok", test names) is written to fd 1
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     130[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   from parallel threads — that text was observed landing *inside* a capture
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     131[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   window and being misreported as a divergence. A small sequential runner
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     132[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   (`common::run_tests`) owns stdout for the whole run instead.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     133[0m:[38;5;10m[49m * Rows that reach `bad()` go through `assert_same_in_process_guarded`, which
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     134[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   probes the configuration in a child process first, so a mistranslation is
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     135[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   reported as a clean failure instead of killing the runner with a `SIGSEGV`.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     136[0m:[38;5;10m[49m * `./check_mutations.sh` applies nine plausible mistranslations and requires the
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     137[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   suite to reject every one, so these rows are demonstrably not vacuous.
